---
/**
 * Four simulations, audited against one schema.
 *
 * The four cards above each describe their model in whatever register suited
 * that project, which reads well one card at a time and makes the four
 * impossible to compare. This asks all of them the same three questions in the
 * same order and prints the answers in columns, so the interesting differences
 * (a seed, a server and a bake, each authoritative over a different thing) are
 * visible by looking down the page rather than by taking my word for it.
 *
 * Ruled and flat, deliberately. Four more raised panels under four raised
 * cards would read as a fifth through eighth project; this is an appendix to
 * the four, and it should look like the back of the book.
 *
 * Closed by default, and it took a while to admit why. Reading across four
 * columns is a thing a reader chooses to do, and until they choose it this is
 * eight hundred pixels of schema between them and the next section. Shut, it
 * is one pill on a rule. The summary names what is inside precisely enough
 * that opening it is a decision rather than a guess.
 *
 * The rows line up across the columns with subgrid, which is what makes
 * reading across a real offer rather than a claim in the lede. Without it the
 * cards still read top to bottom, one project at a time, so the fallback loses
 * the comparison and nothing else.
 */
import { places } from '../data/content';
import { FIELDS, models, NOT_ON_RECORD } from '../data/models';
---

<details class="models notes" data-reveal>
  {
    /* The heading stays a heading inside the control, so the four card names
      below it are still an h4 under an h3 rather than under the section. */
  }
  <summary>
    <h3 class="models__t">Appendix &middot; all four models, asked the same three questions</h3>
  </summary>

  <div class="models__body">
    <p class="models__lede">
      Four simulations, asked in the same order so they can be read across rather than one at a
      time: what decides the outcome, what goes into it, and how I know it is behaving.
    </p>

    <div class="models__grid">
      {
        models.map((m) => (
          <article class="mcard">
            <h4 class="mcard__n">
              <a href={`#${places[m.key].at}`}>{places[m.key].name}</a>
            </h4>
            <p class="mcard__kind">{m.kind}</p>
            <dl class="mcard__f">
              {FIELDS.map(([k, label]) => (
                <div>
                  <dt>{label}</dt>
                  {m.fields[k] ? (
                    <dd>{m.fields[k]}</dd>
                  ) : (
                    /* Not styled as an apology. It is a row of the audit like
                       any other, and the answer it carries is a real one. */
                    <dd class="mcard__gap">{NOT_ON_RECORD}</dd>
                  )}
                </div>
              ))}
            </dl>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </div>
</details>
